WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:06.120
Amazon first hit the public markets in June
of 1997 as a humble online book retailer,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.029
but it didn’t stay that way for long.

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:14.559
They now control 40% of U.S e-commerce, 40%
of the cloud-computing industry and they even

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:17.560
operate a major Hollywood production studio,

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:22.890
If you had invested just $1,000 dollars in
Amazon back in 1997, that investment would

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:29.289
have been worth over $1.2 million dollars
in August of 2018.

00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:34.660
That’s a whopping 120,000% profit in 21
years!

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:39.830
Every investor dreams of getting in on the
ground floor on a success story like this.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:44.460
But if buying low and selling high is the
touchdown, most of the investment world is filled

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.570
with people fumbling the ball left and right.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:52.150
Thankfully failure is often the greatest teacher
so let’s see what lessons we can learn from

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.560
5 of the stranger fad investment crazes in recent history!

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:09.060
It’s 1993, and while I was doing the Running
Man to “Whoop, There It Is"

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:17.940
The Ty Toy Company launched its newest creation: a deliberately
under-stuffed toy animal called a beanie baby!

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.750
Flash the Orca and his 8 original compatriots
showed up on shelves and quickly found themselves

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.630
in the midst of beanie-mania.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:30.570
There were beanie baby magazines and scores
of busted counterfeit rings!

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:35.530
One couple even had to divvy up their beanie
baby stash before a judge as a part of their

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:36.910
divorce settlement.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:41.770
This particular craze is unique because it
was the result of a carefully crafted strategy

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:47.060
by the toy company itself! Ty Warner, the
eccentric billionaire owner proved himself

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.710
a master manipulator of supply and demand.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:55.940
He refused to sell large orders to big box
stores, instead only selling small, controlled

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.260
amounts to independent retailers.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:03.060
New versions would get retired only a few
months later, prompting buyers to panic and

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.410
rush the stores.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:09.300
But Warner knew the bubble wouldn’t last
and in 1999 the company promptly announced

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.160
they were discontinuing.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.520
And while Ty himself won out out to the tune
of a few billion, many adults who bought in

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:21.219
lost their heads and their wallets, sometimes
spending thousands for a single toy.

00:02:21.219 --> 00:02:25.909
Bottom line: Collectibles as an investment
class are extremely volatile.

00:02:25.909 --> 00:02:29.159
Only collect stuff you truly love for its
own sake.

00:02:29.159 --> 00:02:34.299
Style guru Tim Gunn once called the Croc shoe
a “plastic hoof.”

00:02:34.299 --> 00:02:38.410
And while your fashionista cousin might have
wanted to light them on fire, investors got

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:42.290
pretty fired up when they started noticing
some massive numbers.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:47.840
In September of 2006 the Crocs company IPO’ed
around $17 per share.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.129
Just 18 months later, the stock had quadrupled
to more than $68 a share.

00:02:53.129 --> 00:02:58.391
But then came the great recession and share-holders
found themselves crying into their clogs as

00:02:58.391 --> 00:03:02.519
the stock bottomed out at a dismal $1.21
a share.

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:07.109
And while it has recovered somewhat since
then, thanks to a very important british toddler

00:03:07.109 --> 00:03:10.930
named George, it’s never even come close
to its initial heights.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:17.180
So remember, explosive growth, while exciting,
is more often than not a major red flag of

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:18.180
unsustainability.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:23.590
Speaking of explosive growth, a beloved regional
donut chain called Krispy Kreme decided in

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:27.889
the late 90s to go public and start expanding
across the nation.

00:03:27.889 --> 00:03:33.439
Under the ambitious CEO Scott Livengood, the
company went from just 95 stores in 11 states

00:03:33.439 --> 00:03:36.739
to 367 stores in 38 states.

00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:42.640
In just two years their reported net revenue
skyrocketed by 442%.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:48.249
At one point, the number of donuts they made
per week could stretch from New York to L.A.

00:03:48.249 --> 00:03:51.870
Krispy Kreme artifacts were even added to
the Smithsonian museum!

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:55.809
But it wasn’t long before cracks started
to show up in the icing.

00:03:55.809 --> 00:04:01.519
It started with some low earnings reports,
and in 2004 an SEC audit accused the higher-ups

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:02.519
of channel-stuffing.

00:04:02.519 --> 00:04:08.730
That’s when a company forces unneeded product
on stores to inflate sales numbers.

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:13.909
After it was revealed that their profit reports
were off by around $25 million in the wrong

00:04:13.909 --> 00:04:20.169
direction, the stock lost around 80% of it’s
value and CEO Livengood was promptly booted.

00:04:20.169 --> 00:04:23.910
So don’t forget, competent leadership really
matters!

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:28.199
And if the numbers look too good to be true,
there might not be any real dough beneath

00:04:28.199 --> 00:04:29.280
the glaze..

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.280
Funnily enough, that Krispy Kream CEO famously
blamed our next entry for his company’s

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.280
demise.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:41.810
In 1997 Dr. Atkins' New Diet Revolution became
a New York Times bestseller and remained there

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.650
for five years.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:48.620
At the height of it’s fame, 1 in 11 american
adults reported themselves as following a

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:49.819
low-carb diet.

00:04:49.819 --> 00:04:56.009
So it seemed to make sense that Atkins Nutritionals,
was poised to profit big from their proprietary

00:04:56.009 --> 00:04:58.229
line of low-carb snacks and shakes.

00:04:58.229 --> 00:05:03.930
But the famous founder died in 2003 following
complications from a fall.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:08.770
Probably didn’t help when it came out he
also had a history of congestive heart failure

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:10.150
and hypertension.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:15.270
By the mid two thousands, the diet had run
its course and in 2005 they filed for Chapter

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:19.750
11 bankruptcy after reporting a loss of $340
million.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:25.940
Even though the company is still around, it’s
become something of a hot potato, or sorry...meatball...changing

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:28.000
hands numerous times.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.520
Apparently it’s current owner has been trying
to sell it since 2015, with no takers.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.240
Even though the low-carb movement is still
around, cyclical industries like dieting are

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.050
notoriously fickle and no place to look for
long-term returns.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:45.610
Speaking of carbs, let’s look at a “healthier”
alternative.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:48.870
An apple a day keeps the doctor away, right?

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:53.400
And in the 90’s investors were hoping the
Snapple brand would add a healthy boost to

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:54.729
their portfolios.

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:59.260
Their wholesome-feeling branding combined
with a funny, unorthodox spokeswoman nicknamed

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:03.669
“The Snapple Lady” led to an all-out takeover
of the beverage market.

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:07.920
But when the company went public, most of
the money raised by the offering wasn’t

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.639
put to work growing the business but rather
went to pay off debt and line the pockets

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:14.310
of their executives.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:20.370
When the Quaker Oats company purchased Snapple
to the tune of $1.7 billion in 1994, a lot

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.569
of analysts considered it way overpriced.

00:06:22.569 --> 00:06:25.319
And it turned out they were right.

00:06:25.319 --> 00:06:28.509
It was re-sold in 1997 for just $300 million.

00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:33.090
Equating to a loss of roughly $2 million/day.

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:37.560
Snapple eventually merged with Dr. Pepper
brand and is trying once again to capitalize

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:39.340
on their absurdist humor.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:44.490
But people read ingredient labels now and
turns out “the best stuff on earth” is

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:47.750
basically as sugary as its big-soda counterparts.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:52.300
Even the best branding on earth can only float
a company for so long.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:56.340
TV and the internet are filled with people
trying to convince you to hop on bandwagons

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:57.740
left and right.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:03.970
And while investing is a key to growing wealth,
hopping from fad to fad is fraught with danger.

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:06.910
Even seasoned experts get it wrong more often
than not.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:11.990
So if you’re really not the buy-and-hold
type, don’t let FOMO get the best of you!

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:17.689
Start small, commit to becoming a ruthless
researcher and strap in for a wild but hopefully

00:07:17.689 --> 00:07:18.720
fun ride.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.840
And that’s our two cents!

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:27.620
